WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.250 --> 00:00:05.330
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:11.100
One of the main promises of Virtual Reality,
VR in short is enhancing the quality of our

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:12.800
remote interactions.

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:18.090
With VR, we could talk with our colleagues
and beloved ones through telepresence applications

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:22.940
that create a virtual avatar of us - much
like the ones you see here.

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:28.130
Normally, this requires putting sensors all
over our faces to be able to reconstruct the

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:29.330
gestures we make.

00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:35.480
A previous work used a depth camera that was
hanging off of the VR headset, thus having

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:40.239
a better look of the entirety of our face,
while a later work used a mouth camera to

00:00:40.239 --> 00:00:41.730
solve this problem.

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:46.920
This new paper attempts to capture all of
our gestures by using a headset without these

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:52.830
additional complexities by using no more than
three infrared cameras.

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:56.610
No extra devices hanging off of the headset,
nothing.

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:59.310
All of them are built into the headpiece.

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:04.790
This means two key challenges: one is the
fact that the sensor below sees the face in

00:01:04.790 --> 00:01:10.220
an uncomfortable, oblique angle, below you
see exactly the data that is being captured

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:12.040
by the three sensors.

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:18.060
And two, the output of this process should
be a virtual avatar, but it is unclear what

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:23.150
the correspondence between all this data and
the animated character should be.

00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:28.080
So the idea sounds great, the only problem
is that this is near impossible.

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:31.470
So how did the researchers end up doing this?

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:37.380
Well, what they did is they built a prototype
headset with 6 additional sensors.

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:42.670
Now, wearing this headset would perhaps not
be too much more convenient than the previous

00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:44.980
works we’ve looked at a moment ago.

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:49.940
But don’t judge this work just yet, because
this additional information is required to

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:57.070
create the output avatar, and then, the smaller
three-sensor headset can be trained by dropping

00:01:57.070 --> 00:01:58.860
these additional views.

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:05.950
In short, the augmented, more complex camera
is used as a crutch to train the smaller headset.

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:08.429
Amazing idea, I love it.

00:02:08.429 --> 00:02:12.310
Our more experienced Fellow Scholars also
know that there is a little style transfer

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:18.900
magic being done here, and finally, all of
these partial views are then stitched together

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:25.280
into the final avatar.

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:31.650
You can also see here that it smokes the competition,
uses only three sensors, and does all this

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:32.650
in real time.

00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:33.650
Wow.

00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:35.010
What an amazing paper.

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:39.510
If you want to show your friends how you are
about to sneeze in the highest possible quality

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:41.500
video footage, look no further.

